Q: Apache 2.4 - How to deny access to DocumentRoot but allow &#39;trailing slash&#39; access to DirectoryIndex file A: <p>The problem with <code>&lt;Files &quot;/&quot;&gt;</code> is that <code>/</code> is not a <em>basename</em> (last component of filename). The implicit basename of <code>/</code> is <code>.</code> so <code>/</code> actually means <code>/.</code>, which is an absolute path, while <a href="https://httpd.apache.org/docs/2.4/mod/core.html#files" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>&lt;Files …&gt;</code></a> expects a <em>basename</em>. What therefore does work is <code>&lt;Files &quot;.&quot;&gt;</code>, which will match the basename of the current directory and any subdirectory, as <code>&lt;Files &quot;index.html&quot;&gt;</code> matches any file named <code>index.html</code> in the current directory and any subdirectory</p>
<p>Using only '&lt;Files …&gt;' a working configuration would be:</p>
<pre><code>DocumentRoot /var/www
&lt;Directory &quot;/var/www/&quot;&gt;
  Require all denied
  &lt;Files &quot;.&quot;&gt;
    Require all granted
  &lt;/Files&gt;
  &lt;Files &quot;index.html&quot;&gt;
    Require all granted
  &lt;/Files&gt;
&lt;/Directory&gt;
</code></pre>

